From 92da0a97970bcdd7bc444a41fc1b67fabd33ecef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Leonardo Tada Antunes <leonardotada@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 16:32:23 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 06/18] Sweep concurrently with the mutators, on a background
 thread

The stop-the-world part of a cycle (prepare, mark) is unchanged and still runs
on the thread that triggered the collection. The sweep now runs after the
world restarts, on a dedicated GC thread, while threads allocate:

- The bins are emptied while the world is stopped, so a slab is allocated
  from only once the sweep has visited it and put it back. Extents and blocks
  created during the sweep carry the cycle as a tag and are left alone.
- A free into a slab the sweep has not visited only clears the slot; the sweep
  inserts or frees the slab. Dead slots are found without a lock, finalizers
  and poison run outside any lock, and the slab is updated under its bin lock.
- The sweep goes one block at a time and, inside a block, in steps of about
  10 us, so a mutator that wants the page filler lock waits very little.
- The sweeper is paced to 50% of the time (SYMGC_SWEEP_DUTY), unless the heap
  grows past the collection target, and threads that allocate help the
  sweep when it grows past 1.25x the target, so a program that allocates
  faster than the sweeper cannot grow the heap without bound.
- Free pages are kept warm up to the collection target instead of being
  returned to the OS on every cycle.
- Born-after-mark extents are not counted as survivors, so allocating during
  a sweep does not inflate the next target.
- An explicit GC.collect() and a fork wait for a running sweep.

Measured on the gate (Ryzen 7 3800X): worst frame at 512 MiB live 28.9 -> 11.5
ms, at 2 GiB 76 -> 33 ms (the stop-the-world mark); alloc-1t +17%, frag 2.4x
faster; steady RSS of frag +47%. Unit tests (with and without the verifier),
the Phobos stress and the verifier on every workload pass. Finalizers still run
in the sweep and can see slots of other dead objects released earlier in the
same sweep; see the plan.
